                           CHAPTER FIFTEEN


                             The Blade
                T R AC K   “Mama Said Knock You Out” — LL Cool J
                       ECHO     the telephone exchange, 1951




Nobody tells you how good it feels.
    Rafa had said not yet to Mama Tete, and I had said nothing, and I’d
walked home from Sofía’s dining room with the run-of-show folded in
my pocket like a summons. Nine-forty. Eleven minutes. I did what I do
when something scares me: I stayed up and built something.
    This time I built the thing I’d promised myself I wouldn’t.
    It started as a cancellation. That’s the word I used, because it was a
generous word. You take a sound and you make its exact opposite,
same pitch, same pulse, flipped, and you play the two together and
they eat each other. Headphones do it. Cars do it. I had the loop on my
laptop, clean, pulled from the raw feed in March: a hundred and ten
hertz, pulsed at six and a half, eight seconds around. All I had to do
was be its mirror.
    The problem is that to cancel something precisely, you have to be
pointed at it. You have to aim.
    At 2 a.m. I had the mirror. At 3 a.m. I had a better idea of where to
put it. The steam riser in my closet runs the height of the building like a
spine; it had been carrying Aperture’s sound up into my room since
January. A pipe that carries a sound in can carry one out. I patched
Chismosa backward, fed the mirror into the contact mic, and turned a
listener into a speaker.



